<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luo Fangbo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1738">1738</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Lo Fong Pak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luo Fangbo was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> scholar and migrant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> who organised Chinese gold miners in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. In 1777 he founded the Lanfang kongsi at Mandor near Pontianak, a self-governing community of miners with elected leaders, sometimes called the first republic in Asia. Allied with the Pontianak Sultanate, it lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> annexation in 1884.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luo-fangbo.jpg" alt="Luo Fangbo" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luo_Fangbo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Luo Fangbo was born into a family of farmers and students in Jiaying Prefecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, in what is now Meixian District. He studied literature and martial arts and tried his hand at farming and keeping livestock, but for years he remained a poor scholar. In 1772, after failing the provincial examination, he sailed from Humen for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> with more than a hundred relatives and friends, a journey he recounted in a poem, The Journey to Jinshan. After several storms he landed in Borneo, where many Chinese already lived, and at first earned his living as a teacher.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His learning and decisiveness won him the respect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> community, and the local clan associations of the Pontianak area chose him as their leader. In 1777 his standing surpassed that of Wu Yuansheng of the Jusheng Company, a Jiaying man who had fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> after plotting a rising against the Qing; Luo became the company's leader and renamed it the Lanfang Company. At the time more than seven mining companies were fighting one another, and bandits and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> were rife.</p><p>Luo allied with the newly founded Pontianak Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syarif-abdurrahman-alkadrie">Syarif Abdurrahman Alkadrie</a> against rival kongsis and bandits, and the sultan granted him the land of Dongwanlu, modern Mandor, which became the capital of a self-governing community. In 1789 he supported Pontianak's war against the Mempawah Sultanate, and from 1793 the sultan gave him wider authority over the Chinese kongsis.</p><p>Under him the community had its own laws and taxes, trained its people in arms and promoted mining, farming, roads and schools. He sent envoys asking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> to accept it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, but the emperor showed no interest. Luo died of illness in 1795, having named Jiang Wubo as his successor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Luo is the earliest known author of Chinese ekphrasis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, with works such as Ode to the Golden Mountain and Crocodile Writings. Xie Qinggao wrote of him in his Hai Lu of 1820, and in 1905 Liang Qichao listed him among eight great men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s overseas history. In Pontianak temples and a cemetery honour him, and a ceremony is held at his tomb each year on his birthday. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took the region, Resident Cornelis Kater removed Luo's tablets from the kongsi headquarters, provoking the Third Kongsi War, which ended only in September 1885.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luo Fangbo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Luo Fangbo</b>, a Hakka scholar and migrant from Guangdong, organised Chinese gold miners near Pontianak and in 1777 founded the Lanfang kongsi at Mandor.</blockquote><a href="/state/lanfang-republic">Lanfang Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Pontianak, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-0.08,109.37&amp;year=1775" aria-label="Pontianak on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7588 2167 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Luo Fangbo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8038 2437h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8038 2437h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-0.08,109.37&amp;year=1775">What was here in 1775? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
